A mutable receiver borrows an existing place for exclusive access. Put the
tilde before the type in the parameter list and before the receiver at the
call site.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Vector2 = | x F64, y F64 |

    reset |this ~Vector2| {
        ~this.x = 0.0
        ~this.y = 0.0
    }

    vector ~= Vector2(3.0, 4.0)
    ~vector.reset()
]

The two writes change the caller's vector. Mark the whole field access
with `~` when writing through `this`. You can change the fields but can't
replace `this` itself.

A mutable receiver requires a writable place, such as the mutable binding
above. An immutable binding or the temporary result of a constructor can't
grant that access. An ordinary mutable function parameter can accept a
fresh value directly. To call a mutable method on a fresh value, bind it to
a mutable name first.

The call marker follows the declared receiver. A shared method rejects the
tilde and a mutable method requires it. The marker grants access for this
call without moving the value or changing its type.
